  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cam Schlittler held the game. Eight scoreless innings against a quiet offense. Austin Wells broke a tied game in the tenth with a single. That separation cost an Angels team that had just evened things up one inning earlier. The Yankees won because their best pitcher delivered exactly when it mattered most.",
    "what_this_means": "Schlittler entered this game with a 2.04 ERA and exited with a complete argument for why that number means something. Eight innings, two hits, one run. Against a team that could not manufacture anything until Wells gave them air to breathe in the tenth. The offense needed him to hold a 1-1 tie, and he did. By the time they scored five runs in that tenth, the game had already been decided by who could stay composed when nothing was happening. This is a team that doesn't need to lead early. They need a pitcher who doesn't break when the game is locked, and they need hitters who can finish what the pitcher started. Wells delivered both, but Schlittler made it possible. Bullpen continuity. Bednar and Fulmer throwing clean innings behind him. Meant the game stayed tight enough to break open late. That's the only formula worth repeating.",
    "what_to_watch": "Walker Buehler takes the mound for San Diego tomorrow night with a 4.66 ERA. Max Fried comes in with a 1.39 mark across his last four starts and hasn't lost since August. One is carrying his team into September. One is trying to find his way back.",
